{
  "concept": "Wavelength, wavenumber, and energy are inversely related across the electromagnetic spectrum, and organic infrared spectroscopy uses only the narrow 2.5–25 μm (4000–400 cm⁻¹) window of the much broader infrared region.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal spectrum band with ragged torn edges at both ends",
    "six colored segments left to right: Ultraviolet (deep purple), Visible (narrow, medium violet), Near infrared (pale pink), Infrared (wide, stronger pink), Far infrared (very pale pink), Microwaves (pale green)",
    "segment labels: Ultraviolet, Visible, Near infrared, Infrared, Far infrared, Microwaves",
    "wavelength axis labeled λ (cm) with decade ticks at 10⁻⁵, 10⁻⁴, 10⁻³, 10⁻², 10⁻¹",
    "left leader-line annotation: λ = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ cm = 2.5 μm, ν̃ = 4000 cm⁻¹, pointing to the left boundary of the Infrared segment",
    "right leader-line annotation: λ = 2.5 × 10⁻³ cm = 25 μm, ν̃ = 400 cm⁻¹, pointing to the right boundary of the Infrared segment",
    "broad gradient arrow above the band, pale yellow tail at right to deep orange-red head at left, pointing left, labeled 'Energy'",
    "draggable wavelength marker with readouts (λ in cm, μm, wavenumber, relative energy)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "wavelengthSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Wavelength λ (log cm)",
      "range": [-5, -1, 0.05],
      "default": -3.6,
      "teaches": "Dragging toward longer wavelengths lowers both the wavenumber and energy readouts while moving the marker rightward, making the inverse λ–energy and λ–wavenumber relationships physically felt across the same strip"
    },
    {
      "id": "spectrumWindow",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Full spectrum", "Organic IR window"],
      "default": "Full spectrum",
      "teaches": "Organic IR spectroscopy uses only the 2.5–25 μm span (4000–400 cm⁻¹), a narrow slice of the full infrared region — zooming to that window shows how small the analytically useful portion is"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The spectrum and its inverse scales",
      "narration": "The strip runs from ultraviolet (short wavelength) to microwaves (long wavelength), while the Energy arrow above points the opposite way — energy increases as wavelength decreases.",
      "state": { "wavelengthSlider": -5, "spectrumWindow": "Full spectrum" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drag the wavelength marker",
      "narration": "Slide the marker from left to right: the λ and μm values grow while the wavenumber and energy readouts shrink — the same physical position encodes both, always inversely.",
      "state": { "wavelengthSlider": -1, "spectrumWindow": "Full spectrum" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom to the organic IR window",
      "narration": "Switching to the organic IR window zooms into the 2.5–25 μm span bounded by 4000 and 400 cm⁻¹ — the narrow midportion of infrared that organic chemists actually use for structure determination.",
      "state": { "wavelengthSlider": -3.6, "spectrumWindow": "Organic IR window" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Wavelength and energy (and wavenumber) are inversely related, and the entire practice of organic IR spectroscopy lives in one narrow window: 2.5–25 μm, or 4000–400 cm⁻¹.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "A single horizontal band dominates the middle of the figure, with wavy torn edges at both extremes to imply continuation. Six segments with clearly distinct pastel fills: deep purple (Ultraviolet), medium violet (Visible, noticeably narrower than the others), pale pink (Near infrared), stronger pink (Infrared, the widest segment), very pale pink (Far infrared), pale green (Microwaves); each labeled centered in its segment. Below, a log wavelength axis 'λ (cm)' with decade labels 10⁻⁵ through 10⁻¹ increasing left to right. Above, a thick arrow with a yellow-to-orange-red gradient pointing left, labeled 'Energy' — its opposition to the wavelength axis is the central visual idea. Two thin leader lines connect the multi-line annotations (λ in cm, μm conversion, wavenumber) up to the left and right edges of the Infrared segment. The slider marker is a small vertical caret riding the band with a live readout panel showing λ (cm), μm, ν̃ (cm⁻¹), and relative energy; the current segment highlights as the marker enters it. Toggling to 'Organic IR window' zooms/fades the view so only the span between the two leader lines fills the band, with the 4000 and 400 cm⁻¹ labels retained at the ends."
}

